# todo/t1410 — the FULL TYPED AXIS for the wrapping operators:
# 3 operators x 4 widths x {signed, unsigned} = 24 cells.
# Every cell overflows: that is the input these operators exist for.
#
# ⚠ UNARY `-` IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT. `-TYPE_MIN` is signed-overflow UB in C
# too and `Inst::Neg` carries no `Overflow` field at all, but WHAT it should do
# is unratified (`0 - INT64_MIN` traps `T_Overflow`; `-INT64_MIN` silently
# wraps). Its repro is `known_gaps/t1443_neg_overflow.gg`, whose test is
# `#[ignore]`d and asserts the finding rather than sanitizer-cleanliness; the
# ruling is todo/t1443. If the neg cells were here, this fixture's own
# `runtime error:` assertion would be RED ON ARRIVAL.
#
# ⚠ THIS FIXTURE IS NOT BROKEN. It sits in `known_gaps/` FOR PLACEMENT ONLY,
# with a LIVE test (precedent: `callable_amp_struct_iterator_body_writes_through`,
# "Stays in known_gaps/ (out of runtime_parity_corpus) with a LIVE test").
# `runtime_parity_corpus` auto-scans every TOP-LEVEL `tests/fixtures/*.gg`, and
# the SELF-HOST lane still treats every narrow integer as int64 — MEASURED at
# 2026-09-05: the great majority of these cells diverge on the self-host
# (`i8_add` -> 128 not -128, `u8_add` -> 256 not 0, `u64_sub` -> -1 not
# 18446744073709551615). That is todo/t0655's narrow-width gap, open on the
# self-host lane. Top-level placement would book this round's OWN inflow into
# `RUNTIME_DIFF_NONMATCH_CEILING`, which Core #9 forbids. The three 64-bit
# cells that DO match the self-host live top-level in `wrapping_ops_defined.gg`.
#
# ⚠ CONSEQUENCE OF THAT PLACEMENT: `known_gaps/` is an OUT row in
# tests/sanitize/CORPUS_MANIFEST.txt, so `scripts/sanitize_sweep.sh` does NOT
# walk this file. Its UB pin is therefore the LIVE test
# `wrapping_ops_axis_defined` in tests/integration.rs, which builds with
# `--sanitize` and greps stderr for `runtime error:` ITSELF. It must NOT use
# `assert_gg_sanitize_clean`: that helper matches only LeakSanitizer /
# AddressSanitizer / ERROR: / SUMMARY:, while a non-fatal UBSan finding prints
# lowercase `runtime error:` and exits 0 (todo/t1411).
#
# MEASURED CELL TABLE AT HEAD (pre-fix), and the promotion argument stated
# CORRECTLY, because a cell-by-cell promotion argument is exactly where this
# got wrong twice:
#   * 32- and 64-bit operands do NOT promote, so all six signed cells
#     {int32, int64} x {+%, -%, *%} are UB at HEAD.
#   * At 8 and 16 bits the operands promote to `int` and the operation happens
#     there. For `+` and `-` at both widths, and for `*` at 8 bits, the `int`
#     result cannot overflow, so those cells are DEFINED.
#   * `*` AT 16 BITS IS THE EXCEPTION. Signed 16-bit operands cap the product
#     at 2^30, so `i16_mul` is defined at HEAD — but UNSIGNED ones reach
#     65535 * 65535 = 4294836225 > INT_MAX, so `u16_mul` IS UB AT HEAD. "Every
#     unsigned cell is C-defined" is FALSE, and so is "int8/int16 are laundered
#     by promotion" without the `*`-at-16-bits carve-out.
#   * ⇒ SEVEN of the 24 cells are UB at HEAD: the six signed 32/64-bit cells
#     plus `u16_mul`.
#
# ⚠ AND THAT IS WHY THE FIX COMPUTES IN `uint64_t` RATHER THAN IN A SAME-WIDTH
# UNSIGNED COMPANION. A same-width companion would leave `u16_mul` undefined
# AND make `i16_mul` — defined at HEAD — newly undefined, because the
# `uint16_t` cast widens the operand range to [0, 65535]. It would be a NET
# REGRESSION on one cell. `uint64_t` has rank >= `int`, so nothing promotes at
# any width and no per-width argument is needed at all.
#
# ⚠ SIX of those seven are visible to UBSan; `u16_mul` is NOT, because GCC
# narrows the truncated multiply and the diagnostic never appears in the shape
# the backend emits. That cell's instrument is the emitted-shape guard
# `wrapping_arith_computes_in_uint64` in tests/c_runtime.rs. A count of UBSan
# `runtime error:` lines is an INSTRUMENT COUNT, NOT A DEFECT COUNT.
#
# ⚠ WHAT THE VALUE ROWS DO AND DO NOT PIN (do not conclude the guard above is
# redundant). They pin that the computing type is not NARROWER than the
# declared type: narrow `LirType::wrapping_compute_c` to `uint8_t` at every
# width, rebuild, and `i16_add` reads 256 instead of -32768 (`i64_add` reads
# 256 too) -- RED. MEASURED 2026-09-05; regenerate by making that one-line
# change and running this fixture.
# They CANNOT pin that the computing type is WIDE ENOUGH: every value here was
# already byte-identical before the fix, and byte-identical again under a
# SAME-WIDTH companion (measured both directions). No runtime instrument can
# see that; the emitted-shape guard is what does.
#
# ⚠ THE UNSIGNED CELLS LEGITIMATELY WRAP, AND THAT IS NOT todo/t1438. They use
# `+%` / `*%`, whose contract IS wraparound. Plain unsigned `+` currently wraps
# too — on the C lane only, while LLVM traps it — and D30 rules that one wrong;
# that is todo/t1438's divergence and those answers must flip. The rows here
# must not be read as ratifying it.
#
# ⚠ THE OPERANDS MUST BE NON-CONSTANT — `z()` is a runtime-computed 0. With
# literals the whole expression folds and the probe is green for a reason
# unrelated to what it tests (SIX-Q #6).
#
# ⚠ AND THE `i16_mul` / `u16_mul` OPERANDS BELOW (49152 and 98304 at `int`
# width) ARE THREE ORDERS OF MAGNITUDE BELOW `INT_MAX`. A green row here does
# NOT cover the 16-bit multiply boundary; only the emitted-shape guard does.

int z():
    String s = ""
    return s.byte_len()


int8 i8(int x):
    return x as int8


int16 i16(int x):
    return x as int16


int32 i32(int x):
    return x as int32


uint8 u8(int x):
    return x as uint8


uint16 u16(int x):
    return x as uint16


uint32 u32(int x):
    return x as uint32


uint64 u64(int x):
    return x as uint64


void main():
    int k = z()

    # ── signed, 64-bit (int) ──
    print(f"i64_add={9223372036854775807 + k +% (1 + k)}")
    print(f"i64_sub={-9223372036854775807 - 1 + k -% (1 + k)}")
    print(f"i64_mul={(4611686018427387904 + k) *% (3 + k)}")

    # ── signed, 32-bit ──
    print(f"i32_add={i32(2147483647 + k) +% i32(1 + k)}")
    print(f"i32_sub={i32(-2147483648 + k) -% i32(1 + k)}")
    print(f"i32_mul={i32(1073741824 + k) *% i32(3 + k)}")

    # ── signed, 16-bit ──
    print(f"i16_add={i16(32767 + k) +% i16(1 + k)}")
    print(f"i16_sub={i16(-32768 + k) -% i16(1 + k)}")
    print(f"i16_mul={i16(16384 + k) *% i16(3 + k)}")

    # ── signed, 8-bit ──
    print(f"i8_add={i8(127 + k) +% i8(1 + k)}")
    print(f"i8_sub={i8(-128 + k) -% i8(1 + k)}")
    print(f"i8_mul={i8(64 + k) *% i8(3 + k)}")

    # ── unsigned, 64-bit ──
    print(f"u64_add={u64(-1 + k) +% u64(1 + k)}")
    print(f"u64_sub={u64(0 + k) -% u64(1 + k)}")
    print(f"u64_mul={u64(-9223372036854775807 - 1 + k) *% u64(3 + k)}")

    # ── unsigned, 32-bit ──
    print(f"u32_add={u32(4294967295 + k) +% u32(1 + k)}")
    print(f"u32_sub={u32(0 + k) -% u32(1 + k)}")
    print(f"u32_mul={u32(2147483648 + k) *% u32(3 + k)}")

    # ── unsigned, 16-bit ──
    print(f"u16_add={u16(65535 + k) +% u16(1 + k)}")
    print(f"u16_sub={u16(0 + k) -% u16(1 + k)}")
    print(f"u16_mul={u16(32768 + k) *% u16(3 + k)}")

    # ── unsigned, 8-bit ──
    print(f"u8_add={u8(255 + k) +% u8(1 + k)}")
    print(f"u8_sub={u8(0 + k) -% u8(1 + k)}")
    print(f"u8_mul={u8(128 + k) *% u8(3 + k)}")
